Got it, ties break by first appearance in the input, not alphabetically. So I'll track the first index each word appears at, and when counts tie, the smaller index wins. Let me code that up.

```javascript
function top_k_words(words, k) {
  const count = new Map();
  const firstSeen = new Map();

  words.forEach((word, i) => {
    count.set(word, (count.get(word) || 0) + 1);
    if (!firstSeen.has(word)) firstSeen.set(word, i);
  });

  const distinct = [...count.keys()];
  distinct.sort((a, b) => {
    const diff = count.get(b) - count.get(a);
    return diff !== 0 ? diff : firstSeen.get(a) - firstSeen.get(b);
  });

  return distinct.slice(0, k);
}
```

Counting is O(n), and sorting the distinct words is O(d log d) where d is the number of distinct words. If k is small I can swap the sort for a bounded heap of size k to get O(n log k), but I'd keep the same tie-break comparator. Want me to write that version?

